WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.840
Film Courage: Erik, you have a book that's coming out
right now we're filming this in August

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.350
but by the time this airs it'll be
September late September I believe and

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:13.230
your book is called The idea: Seven
Elements of a Viable Story for Screen,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:18.330
Stage or Fiction. Curious what prompted
you to write the book? Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: Yeah so I've been

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.430
a screenwriter for like 25 years and for
the last about ten years I've been

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.640
teaching screenwriting I've been working
one-on-one with a lot of writers as like

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.869
a consultant or coach and I've been just
reading a lot of scripts film and TV

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:35.550
scripts many many many many many scripts
right so you in trying to help writers

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.329
and to try to kind of codify principles
of writing which is always a touchy

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:41.969
thing because sometimes people feel
writing can't really be taught or

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:47.640
shouldn't be taught you know I've really
tried to figure out what are the things

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.590
that one really needs and a story idea
because people don't necessarily seem to

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.370
know when they're writing but when
someone reads someone's script they

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:01.440
often are having negative reactions and
what are the reasons for those reactions

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.949
I really made it my business to figure
out like what are the sort of essential

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:08.430
things that people are reacting to not
only when they read a script but when

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.240
they even hear the idea for a script
because the reason the book is called

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.680
the idea is that I've really come to
believe that the most important part of

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.200
the process most writers bypass too
quickly which is selecting the idea and

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.009
understanding what makes a viable idea
that is so important to the eventual

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.240
success of any project and I mean that
might seem obvious but the average

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.920
writer myself included tends to want to
get to writing they tend to want to just

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.689
start structuring outlining and writing
the script so you pick an idea you often

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:47.640
don't vet the idea with professionals or
friends or anyone you just kind of like

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.479
I sort of like this idea I think I'm
gonna write this and then you go about

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:56.490
writing it whereas in the industry if
you have a manager or an agent what I've

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.450
learned is that they'll want you to run
your ideas past them before you even

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.420
start one they don't want you to spend
your time and energy on something they

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.520
don't think is viable in the marketplace
to begin with so if you're trying to

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.630
write for that marketplace if you're
trying to get or maintain a

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.490
you're an agent and move forward you
know in like the Hollywood marketplace

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.700
what's gonna happen to you is your
representatives are gonna shoot down a

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.720
lot of your ideas which has happened to
me and they're gonna really stop you at

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.920
the idea stage and want to hear the
logline or the basic the basic premise

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:36.600
or pitch so I as a writer having agents
had to live that lifestyle of I got to

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.560
impress my agents first right once I'm
lucky enough to have one as someone

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.450
reading people's scripts as a kind of
consultant or teacher I now realized

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.770
what my agents were thinking because
most of the things people bring to me

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:53.580
finish scripts the most of the notes
that I have on their scripts are notes I

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.880
would have had on the basic idea if they
had just brought it to me when it was a

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.920
log line or a 30-second pitch or a
one-page synopsis the kind of you put in

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.680
a query so but most writers don't kind
of realize that because they haven't

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.530
vetted the idea with anyone so I've been
blogging for five to ten years now you

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.340
know little tips and things that little
pieces of wisdom that I feel I've come

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.150
up with having read all these scripts
and seeing the kind of things that I see

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:25.080
writers are doing and the blog
eventually led to the idea to do a book

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.910
and the book is about let's focus on the
idea what makes a viable idea it's not a

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.450
book about the whole process of
screenwriting it's not about story

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.470
structure per se it's not about writing
scenes it's not about navigating the

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:42.270
business although all those things are
touched on it is exclusively about what

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:47.400
makes a viable idea and let's slow down
writers and really work on getting the

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.470
idea right before we you know write the
script or even outline the script so

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:58.650
what makes a viable idea so I came up
with this little acronym of the word

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.240
problem because I feel that every story
is really about a problem and it's all

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.750
about you know when someone's are
reacting to your script or your pitch or

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:12.120
your logline what they're mostly
thinking consciously or not is okay

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.940
what's the problem with the center of
this story does this problem sound

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.090
really compelling and entertaining to
watch is an audience gonna care about

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.830
this problem that this main character or
these characters are trying to solve

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.790
whether it's film television or even
commercial fiction or theater

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.990
I think the same kind of basic premises
of you know how story and ideas for

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:36.890
story work applies to all them which is
why the title mentions stage and fiction

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:40.710
they're looking at what the problem is
what's the nature of the problem or

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:45.000
what's the nature of the goal for the
main character right so I took problem

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.560
and I created this acronym from the
letters in the word problem and the book

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.820
is basically a presentation of these
seven elements that start with those

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.380
seven letters and those are the elements
that I think successful commercially

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.760
viable stories that would interest an
age and a manager a producer and editor

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.880
etc tend to have you want to hear what
they are so it's just quickly and we can

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.450
go deeper if you want the problem at the
center of the story needs to be

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:23.750
punishing relatable original believable
life-altering entertaining and

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.930
meaningful so some of those words I
could have used different words but they

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.770
wouldn't fit the problem so I kind of
made it so they all lined up with those

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:37.950
seven letters that's great and and did
you before sort of even formulating the

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.270
idea for this book no pun intended go
through other scripts and really hone in

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.040
on what's the problem let's say some of
the blockbusters or maybe even indie

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.370
films what really is the core of this
problem and then work backwards well

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.520
yeah I do that all the time it's just a
natural thing whenever I'm watching

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.020
anything I am assessing what is the
central to me that's what the story is

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:02.070
what's the central problem here we could
talk examples if you wanted but pretty

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.330
much any genre it's about characters who
are kind of punished usually as they're

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:10.920
trying to resolve some situation and so
it's just becomes second nature to me to

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:16.290
think in terms of what's the problem at
the center of any TV series TV episode

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.370
movie story in general and of course
when I look at someone's script or I

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.910
just see the log line even I'm like I
said I'm looking at what's the problem

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.150
here and sometimes the log line doesn't
even make the problem clear or it

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.630
focuses too much on the internal problem
for the characters because sometimes

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:36.120
writers confuse like internal character
arc with external problem and great

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.260
stories generally have both but the
external problem is kind of the part

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.280
that people really want to know about
when they are assessing your idea it's

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.430
like the external problem has to be
really solid the internal arc is a

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:51.870
little more optional but you've got to
have that big external problem typically

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:56.790
in a commercial type project and so
that's what I'm talking about more than

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:00.480
the internal what the character needs to
learn and how they need to grow stuff

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.070
which is the arc or the theme or the
flaw you know that kind of stuff

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:09.750
sometimes we writers tend to focus a lot
on that and make that drive all of our

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:13.680
efforts and what I've learned is it's it
tends to be better to let that stuff

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.850
stay a little flexible until you've
gotten the sort of external problem

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:22.140
worked out and even kind of structured
out a bit because sometimes your sense

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.290
of what the theme is or what the
characters growth should be will change

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:29.430
and shift once you've really explored
the external problem the external

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:33.330
challenge that they're you know every
scene is typically about them grappling

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:37.350
with and trying to resolve I love that
you've chosen the word punishing whether

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:41.070
it was just coincidence because it
starts with P but because you're putting

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.790
yourself in the protagonists shoes and
it seems like if you're looking through

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.810
life through the lens of that character
that it is a punishment for you know

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.350
that that they are enduring things that
are unfair to them so I like that you've

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:57.540
chosen that as because it really is sort
of the journey of it of a character to

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.020
see it as this is a punishment now I've
got to prove that I'm innocent kind of

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.890
thing thank you yeah I mean I I think
even in even in comedy series on

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:09.090
television one thing I often find myself
reminding myself in my own projects and

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:13.140
telling other writers that come to me
and want feedback and stuff is that your

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.520
characters should really be in hell and
under siege pretty much all the time

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.180
like even if you watch a show like 30
rock means they're slightly dated

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.240
references now but like Everybody Loves
Raymond but I could also talk about like

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.800
veep or Glo or current series if you
really look at what's going on for the

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.210
characters that we're following it's
basically their suffering they're under

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.840
siege they're in a sort of hell and
they're trying to get out of it pretty

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:41.430
much every episode for us as an audience
it's fun to watch it's fun to watch

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:45.510
their reactions to things and just sort
of how their characters operate and

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:50.460
interact in a comedy in a drama or a
thriller it's fun a different way for

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:56.240
the audience it's fun for us it's never
for them you know even in even in the

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.470
you know the the save the cat book the
Blake Schneider book he talks about the

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:05.330
fun in game section which is the first
half of Act two in a screenplay in his

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:09.410
world and I think he makes some great
points but the fun and game section even

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.370
in that section of the movie which he
calls flooding games I'm all often

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:17.120
telling people I don't think your
characters should so much be having fun

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.320
or enjoying the situation your
characters are kind of under siege even

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.530
in the first half of Act two they're
kind of struggling suffering being

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:28.700
punished by the situation they're in the
upside-down world they find themselves

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.600
in the problem or goal that they're
trying to resolve which they're an

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.830
underdog and they're overmatched and the
world is not giving them what they want

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:44.690
that's pretty much every story in my
view in every genre so it's it's it's

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.530
interesting how often writers tend to
write scenes of characters kind of

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:54.050
getting along and kind of having
victories and ice and I usually say that

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.440
you know in a story the victories
generally only come at the very end if

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.860
they come anywhere other than the very
end they're usually very short-lived and

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:06.170
often overshadowed by the bigger problem
that still demands resolution because

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.770
what keeps the drama or the comedy
moving is that sense of there's a

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:12.980
problem I'm trying to solve this problem
there's this goal I'm trying to reach

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:16.490
this goal problem and goal are kind of
two sides of the same thing

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:21.710
some stories the goal is just to solve
the problem in other stories the goal is

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:25.910
its own positive thing and the problem
is I haven't reached the goal so it's

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:30.740
kind of like problem / goal I think of
it as like the same thing but that being

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:36.650
difficult and unresolved and also
changing and evolving and usually

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.370
getting worse and more complicated over
the course of the story or the TV

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:45.770
episode or the TV series is kind of this
key thing that I'm always looking at

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:50.210
many audiences and certainly industry
professionals are looking at and

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:56.000
evaluating consciously or not do I feel
the problem is compelling and big is it

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.720
getting worse you know are the
characters struggling is it changing and

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.880
evolving but generally in a direction of
worse and more difficult until the kind

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.450
of final battle at the end where
the resolution finally happens so that's

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.340
why problem is that word that I think
I'm always referring to when I'm talking

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:20.040
about a story so you say that writers
don't spend enough time making sure that

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.570
the idea that they're sort of putting
out there is viable wouldn't you you

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:27.900
think that most writers are using an
idea that's very personal to them in

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.010
some sense even if it's the story's not
really about them it's a core idea that

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:37.320
affects them why wouldn't it translate
if it's so personal and deep and rich to

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:41.460
that writer wouldn't putting something
so sort of deep and personal that the

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:46.170
writer is choosing a focus on be enough
to make the script viable well that's a

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.900
great question first let me say when I
talk about writers I'm talking about

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:52.620
myself too because I'm a writer and
that's still the main thing I do and I

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:56.370
make these things that I might call
mistakes so it's not me on HIGH saying

00:11:56.370 --> 00:12:00.750
oh those writers what idiots they hire
its me saying we all naturally do this

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:05.430
stuff I just wanna make that clear to
your audience sure but that's a great

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:08.790
question and and I've made I don't I
wouldn't called a mistake but I've done

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:13.650
this myself many times which is write
something that is personal to me that I

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:19.230
think I felt something deeply about so
audiences will - and one often we know

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:23.550
one hopes they can pull that off and
there are some pitfalls in that approach

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:29.460
that often happened one of them being
that we so internalize what we went

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:32.790
through what we were thinking and
feeling and why it mattered so much to

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:38.760
us in our life to put it actually on the
page in a way that audience is looking

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:43.950
at a character based on us having
experiences based on ours for the

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:47.250
audience to get those things that are
internal to us is harder than it looks

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.839
and often just because we felt it deeply
doesn't mean that we can write a

00:12:51.839 --> 00:12:56.070
character feeling that same thing deeply
and the audience will get it sometimes

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:00.000
they will sometimes those are the best
movies but a lot of times they won't and

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.060
a lot of times that thing that we went
through in our personal life that

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:07.230
mattered so much to us doesn't
necessarily have the ingredients of an

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.460
entertaining to watch story for an
audience like a movie or television

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.060
audience a story that builds and
complicates and gets worse and are

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.950
really fun to watch way often when we're
writing real life and this is any kind

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.320
of true
story and I've worked a lot on true

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.650
stories and people come to me a lot with
true story because I worked on Band of

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:30.870
Brothers for instance it's it's kind of
where I got my start professionally what

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.830
I've really learned is that true stories
are sometimes harder than fiction

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:42.030
because you real life doesn't have story
structure typically real life doesn't

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:46.890
have the things that we writers have
learned that audiences need to be really

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:51.420
emotionally invested and entertained and
to stay emotionally invested entertained

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:55.680
over the course of an hour half hour or
two hours it just doesn and writers have

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:02.280
to bring a lot of manipulation to a true
story to make it comprehensible to an

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:06.390
audience and to make it be that kind of
emotional journey with that kind of

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:09.780
classic narrative build to it and
entertainment value to it it just

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:14.010
doesn't come naturally we have to kind
of impose it so a lot of times the

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:17.850
things we've gone in our own life we
don't have perspective on them we can't

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:21.720
see them from that third party person
that has a very different life from us

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:26.130
and how do we portray it on the page or
on screen in a way that they will be

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:30.930
really entertained and invested in this
character like us and often we aren't

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:35.850
able to write that character with enough
perspective and enough clarity so that

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:39.690
people will get what they're going
through because to us it's already so

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:44.910
obvious it's like a given it's inside of
our head but getting it outside of our

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.840
head and onto the page in a way that
others will really get it I think it's

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.720
harder than it looks
and so when you're identifying an idea

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:56.850
for a story coming from a place that is
something passionate to you or that you

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:00.840
have experienced I think is a good start
but it's usually not enough you usually

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:04.440
have to sort of test it by putting
yourself in the shoes of a sort of third

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:09.360
party anonymous viewer with a different
life story than yours how do you make

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.500
this something that's that's universal
because our own pain in our own drama

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:19.250
and our own situations don't necessarily
have deep and compelling universally

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:24.720
relatable elements to them that any one
or millions of people would be able to

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:29.340
instantly grasp and be emotionally
invested I use that term a lot of

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.070
emotional investment because to me
that's the key thing we're always going

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:34.230
for there's an audience that's the key
thing

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:39.360
you want we want to care and a lot of
this focus on the idea is about finding

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.350
an idea that people you know people
strangers millions of strangers will

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.280
have a chance of really caring about
this idea caring about this character

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.960
these characters in this situation what
makes them care and so these seven

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:57.120
elements a lot of it is about me trying
to put into words here are the qualities

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:02.730
that I think are inherent in stories
that can make an audience care and when

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:05.280
it's coming from our own life experience
a lot of times some of those elements

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:11.760
could be missing and we may not realize
it when a writer brings their work to

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:15.750
you or even from hearing from agents
who've had a writer bring work to them

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:21.990
what sort of telltale signs appear when
it's so deeply personal and you can tell

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:26.460
that that writer doesn't have enough
perspective where is when you can maybe

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:30.870
tell that it's sort of loosely based on
a multitude of people and there's a

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:36.050
little more definition to the problem or
maybe there's not and I'm just

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:40.290
projecting that yeah I don't know if
there are telltale signs because you

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:44.550
never know when you're reading something
is this the author's own life story I

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:49.590
mean you can suspect it but you know
some you know I would say the absence of

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:52.650
some of these elements we're talking
about the like the seven elements of

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:57.210
what the problem you know the absence of
those is the main thing that a reader is

00:16:57.210 --> 00:17:02.190
noticing that it isn't that entertaining
to watch or it isn't the stakes aren't

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:06.930
life-altering it doesn't seem like or
the character isn't relatable or the

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:11.550
problem isn't punishing enough or what's
going on isn't believable enough you

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:16.050
know those kind of things are what we're
just noticing right away once we notice

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:21.240
them we may speculate that oh this is
because it's their own true story and

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:24.990
their own true life situation doesn't
have those elements but we don't really

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:29.550
know for sure so I don't know that I can
say oh I can tell this is like a too

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:34.350
personal true story thing necessarily
unless I really know the writer and it's

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:38.460
pretty obvious that it's their life it's
more that the sort of absence of some of

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:42.840
those elements not appearing as strongly
as we would want makes us less enthused

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:47.250
about the script and it may or may not
be because it was a personal you

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:52.410
no story like that do you remember the
first feedback you received from a third

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:57.900
party that these elements needed to be
more pronounced was that for you

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:02.820
devastating and terrible in the first
moment of my life of twit what I guess I

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.820
had a pretty good life if the worst
moment of my life is having someone you

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:10.470
know tear apart a script but I mean yes
some of the early scripts that I wrote I

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:14.490
would probably cringe at now that I mean
some of the stuff I write now I probably

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:18.660
cringe at six months this is not an easy
feel that's what I'm saying

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:21.690
you always have perspective on what
you're writing yourself and whether

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:25.740
others are gonna respond well to it but
you know I had people say there's just

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:29.700
not enough conflict
it needs more conflict and I in my early

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:34.830
days thought conflict meant like people
arguing or fighting or car chases or

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:40.020
something like I didn't really know what
that meant exactly and so I didn't write

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:43.290
scripts and I didn't love movies that
were all about people constantly arguing

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:47.040
or fighting or car chasing right so I
thought I was writing something that was

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:51.630
the kind of stuff I would like but a
third party reading it said you know you

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.870
did some good things here I just felt it
needed a more conflict I didn't really

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:58.230
know what they meant now after doing it
for years and reading a lot of scripts I

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:03.330
know what they meant and to me conflict
is synonymous with problem so it's not

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:08.040
that your character has to always be in
an obvious conflict with other people

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:12.600
and expressing and fighting all the time
or arguing it's that they have to be in

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:16.980
conflict with their situation meaning
they have to have problems whenever your

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:20.580
character doesn't have a problem and
isn't actively even and struggling

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.900
against or trying to resolve a problem
the audience tends to check out a bit

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:30.150
right so that usually does lead to
conflicts with other characters trying

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:34.170
to solve a problem in life generally
leads to some interpersonal calm because

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:38.130
a lot of problems that are big enough
for a movie that we want to solve or big

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:42.240
enough for a TV show are problems that
involve relations and interactions with

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:46.380
other people so there will be conflict
but it's not that limited version of

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:51.510
what I thought conflict meant when I got
that critique so the problem not being

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:55.320
punishing enough which is like the
letter P the first one in the you know

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.610
the word problem in my book was probably
the first thing that I grappled with in

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:02.520
my own stuff of people
saying it's just not enough conflict

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:06.419
meaning it's not a big enough problem
it's maybe not a life-altering enough

00:20:06.419 --> 00:20:09.960
problem which is the l word which is
about the level of the stakes of the

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:13.320
problem why does it matter that they
solve this problem why does the audience

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.190
care whether they solve it or not is it
big enough and is it relatable enough

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.340
which is the R word where the audience
is starting to feel like it's our

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:25.320
problem too we are standing in the shoes
of this character as they try to solve

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.710
this problem and we feel something about
it we want it to be solved because it's

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.700
kind of like it's us now that emotional
engagement with the character is like

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.570
the really key thing we're trying to
achieve as writers that was not easy to

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:40.679
achieve and sometimes writers don't
realize that things necessary to kind of

00:20:40.679 --> 00:20:46.020
achieve that which is a lot of the kind
of relatable stuff so my first critique

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:49.649
of something not punishing enough was
probably also wasn't big enough stakes

00:20:49.649 --> 00:20:53.760
why do people relate to this character
because you didn't find that sort of

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.409
universal hook that pulled people in the
character didn't have those elements

00:20:58.409 --> 00:21:03.750
that make the audience just start to
feel like I'm bonded with them which is

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:08.279
such a key part for me like the
beginning of any screenplay what save

00:21:08.279 --> 00:21:12.210
the cat calls the setup section I think
the first 10 pages it's all about

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:16.860
achieving that bonding with the audience
and and you know people often talk about

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:20.309
you know people put down a script in the
first 10 pages if it's not grabbing them

00:21:20.309 --> 00:21:25.740
and writers tend to think oh you grab
them by having really exciting spectacle

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:31.230
in the beginning with really high stakes
what action or entertainment value which

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:35.039
is always helpful but to me the more
important goal in those first 10 pages

00:21:35.039 --> 00:21:40.169
is to introduce your main character at
some length and it's some breadth and

00:21:40.169 --> 00:21:44.159
some depth in such a way that the
audience starts to see what the key

00:21:44.159 --> 00:21:48.840
things are that would make us care about
them what makes them a compelling

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.940
relatable person even before the main
story problem has kicked in what makes

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:58.140
us just kind of want to follow them you
know I mean save the cat says they save

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:02.760
a cat which makes us like them because
they do something self-sacrificing and

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.200
helpful that's a sort of semi joke you
know premise I think that Blake Snyder

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:10.200
came about just have your character save
a cat but I think what he was really

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:14.130
getting out was a really vital thing
that writers tend to sometimes bypass or

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.900
no
understand which is give us a reason to

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:22.360
bond with that character before your
story really kicks in and so I probably

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:26.890
failed in all those and other ways in my
early scripts and maybe some later

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:33.370
scripts that made people go huh needs
more conflict so what about the opposite

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:36.669
what if someone says well I'm never
gonna get notes like that I'm gonna have

00:22:36.669 --> 00:22:40.539
it packed with conflict and then it's
actually too much it's not subtle it's

00:22:40.539 --> 00:22:44.230
not under the surface like I'm just
thinking of sharp objects I watched the

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:50.590
first episode last night and you can see
seeds planted that the conflict some of

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:54.789
its obvious some of its not and you're
wondering hmm there was a look at the

00:22:54.789 --> 00:22:57.610
end with this one character I think
there's good you know and I like hints

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:01.030
at it and then it keeps you hooked in
because you're like I want to see

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:06.159
something go down here but it doesn't
yet yeah well I mean yeah it's like it's

00:23:06.159 --> 00:23:10.270
not that you have to hit over hit people
over the head with with constant like

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:14.799
you know screaming matches or fights or
anything like that to me it's that there

00:23:14.799 --> 00:23:18.820
has to be a central problem and a
central character faced with that

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:23.470
problem that the audience buys into
early on and says okay I care about this

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:27.690
problem I want to see this problem get
solved for this person by this person

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:34.659
once you have them feeling that way then
you can play out the resolution of that

00:23:34.659 --> 00:23:38.950
problem over a lengthy period of time
you don't have to get right to it in a

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:43.600
really you know sledgehammer kind of
manner but the the problem has to be big

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:47.950
enough to begin with it has to be grabby
enough to begin with then how they

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:51.220
evolved in dealing with the problem and
how the problem evolves and if it's a

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:56.140
mystery how the cards get turned over
and the clues get revealed and the leads

00:23:56.140 --> 00:24:00.220
get followed up on that's part of the
fun for the audience is that that stuff

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:03.940
takes time you just don't want to have
long periods where none of that's

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:08.260
happening at all and things seem kind of
fine that's what you want to avoid

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:13.480
things are never fine in a story there's
always that simmering you know problem

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:17.590
overshadowing everything I think in a
compelling commercial story where the

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:21.309
audience is going I want to see this
resolved the reason I'm binging on this

00:24:21.309 --> 00:24:25.059
is I want to see this problem or these
problems get resolved but what they

00:24:25.059 --> 00:24:27.670
really want to see is they want to see
how the problems

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:32.440
build and complicated twist and turn and
evolve and then later get resolved

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:35.980
because you know once it gets resolved
it's kind of over so if you're binging

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:40.840
on 12 episodes or whatever it is you
probably know no real resolution gonna

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:45.730
happen til the end of the twelfth if
then but on the way how its evolving is

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:50.470
the thing that's fun for us to watch as
an audience have you given notes to

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:55.360
someone - hey tone it down this is too
much right here it's too explosive and

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:59.200
it's like almost like beating the the
the reader over the head a little bit I

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:02.560
don't think that happens very much
unless what they're doing doesn't feel

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:07.390
believable to me that's the sort of the
be--and problem is you know believable

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:11.470
and and that's a bigger more common
issue than people might think where

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.620
readers are reading their stuff and
going I don't believe this character

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:19.120
would do or say this or maybe it doesn't
it feels contrived in some way it feels

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:23.410
like the writer is trying to force
something that isn't organically coming

00:25:23.410 --> 00:25:27.040
out of the lives and desires and
emotions and personalities of the

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.850
characters so on to some extent the
characters do have to drive the story

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:35.290
everything they're doing has to feel
like it could and would really happen

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:40.810
they could or would really say or do
those things in those moments so it's

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:43.810
not that I'm ever saying oh there's too
much conflict you're trying to make the

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:49.560
problem too big per se unless in doing
that it's straining believability

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:54.550
because even those stories have easily
something exaggerated about them or even

00:25:54.550 --> 00:25:57.880
sometimes a fantastical element that
they're based on like there's a zombie

00:25:57.880 --> 00:26:01.810
apocalypse or there's vampires in the
high school or whatever even though they

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:05.920
have that kind of stuff sometimes and
even in a comedy you're exaggerating

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:10.330
characters a little bit for comedy I
believe there still has to be this basis

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:14.500
of reality that we're seeing human
beings that we can accept seem real

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:19.240
doing or saying what we think they would
really do or say in those situations and

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:22.870
a lot of times in a lot of scripts there
will be moments and scenes and

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:26.790
characters that don't feel that way that
feel over-the-top

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:32.980
unbelievable reacting in ways that seem
forced for the sake of more conflict or

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:37.690
more comedy or entertainment value or
more action so that's where I might

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:41.050
sometimes feel that something needs to
be toned down

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:45.760
so that it feels like it's all coming
from a real place when in doubt always I

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:50.500
say go for the real I heard somebody say
this once a professional writer at at a

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:55.030
panel and it's so true
you hope that your concept is compelling

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:58.510
enough people are gonna care there's
something entertaining at its core but

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:03.010
as you start plotting it out I always
say start with what's real at every

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:06.370
moment in the story what would each
character be really thinking feeling

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:09.820
doing at this point given their
situation

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:14.910
how do you make them do things that feel
totally believable and just totally real

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:20.950
audiences love it when it feels real but
still is entertaining and compelling and

00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:24.550
all those other things and achieving
that level of realness that feels really

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:30.250
authentic is is difficult but it's
really special when a writer is able to

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:35.680
just write with like authenticity that
is just like I absolutely suspend

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.970
disbelief you absolutely have me I
believe these people are real I forget

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:45.400
that I'm watching something fictional so
when you can do that in the context of

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:49.000
having these other elements of a story
then I think you're really in good shape

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.090
can you think of a film in particular
that there was like one of the first

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:58.090
that did that for you well I mean I
think most really great and successful

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:04.030
movies and TV shows do have reality do
you have that basis of reality which is

00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:07.570
why they work if they didn't have it I
don't think they'd be successful so I

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:11.800
think you see that in most successful
things that there is that sense of I

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:14.920
believe these people and what they're
doing I believe the reality the

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.670
situation's the writer has set up having
said that there are certain shows

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:22.810
certain movies that I can think of maybe
not the first that did it but that feel

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:28.300
they did it to a really extreme level
like the wire on HBO that series you

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:32.170
know to me kind of made every other cop
show that it ever come before seemed

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:37.230
almost kind of like cheesy or fake in
comparison because it felt so real and

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:43.750
when you can achieve that level of
realness audiences critics agents

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:48.190
producers they all respond to that if
you have these other kind of elements of

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:53.710
story in place that level of realness
can really make your stuff special so

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:57.250
that's one example the spray
was also a show that even though it was

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:03.190
a real as the wire when I would watch
The Sopranos I would kind of like just

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:06.610
buy it for some reason that was a show
that I felt like I don't feel like I'm

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:11.020
watching actors and writers and
directors I feel like I'm watching these

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:15.280
like real people that are really living
in this subculture at its best which i

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:19.090
think is the reason why shows like The
Sopranos and The Wire are voted like

00:29:19.090 --> 00:29:23.350
some of the best written shows of all
time because they obviously did that for

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:28.660
a lot of people even Forrest Gump I mean
I could I could think that that is a

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:33.100
real person that if you go on Wikipedia
though oh there he is and you know yes

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:39.490
and he beat this person in ping-pong and
I mean it's believable enough as wild of

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:44.260
a ride as his life was that that could
be a real person there's just something

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:49.650
about the the writing and and drawing
you into this sort of innocent world

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:53.770
yeah I mean I think you're right I mean
it's yeah it's a very opposite example

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:59.560
where it's a very fanciful story but
somehow it convinces the audience for

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:03.580
